 right on the spot real time  what are some of your favorite of those  kind of more informal things that  teachers do to start to understand where  students are and what their skills are  oh that's a great question i love math  language routines and so i think about  a math language routine of the problem  with three reads for example so we're  working on a math problem i'm simply  going to start off by asking you to read  the problem and then i'm going to ask  you what is this problem asking us about  so and simply asking that question i'm  getting an understanding of how my  students are interpreting that and then  we might read the question again and  then i'll say okay well what information  do we need in order to solve this  problem so that gives me insight into  the student's level of readiness in  terms of  you know background knowledge that they  might need  and so it's really helping me to unpack  the context and the purpose from my  students understanding teachers are  generally very well skilled at these  strategies that we might use another  thing we might do our entrance and exit  wait i think you said three reads is  there a third read did i hear the third  reading  okay let's go back so the three reasons  let's go back  problem and then i say okay what is this  problem about  and then you're telling me what you  think the problem is about  not confirming or affirming i just want  to know um the second read now i ask you  what are we trying to find out  so a little different there  you know what parents can use appearance  i hope you're taking note what are we  trying to find out and then we read the  the problem again and now i'm going to  ask you what information  might be useful to solve this so in  knowing the information now we're  starting to get what the math is behind  that  yeah that makes sense yes yes  then that sounds like the teacher would  get a lot of information about what the  student  knows and what they can do and all of  those from 