ht in a  classroom where you have students who  their literacy level  is pretty much non-existent versus  students who are just need to be  challenged and since a higher level math  i i have been there okay so i understand  the  um the immediate reaction to first go to  like the reality of the fact that i have  to cover so much content with all of  these students and i think if we start  at that point it feels too overwhelming  it can feel un pretty daunting that's a  daunting task so i think it's important  for our teachers to really get clear on  what is it that my students need to know  what's the grade level standard and what  are the expectations that i personally  hold  for my students um  you know i had just mentioned the  interrupted and unfinished learning  sometimes kristin when we have a lot of  interrupted learning it can lead to  assumptions  meaning  i can very easily assume  that my students  don't know  absc content simply because of the fact  that they miss so much  um and i think when we have assumptions  like that i have a colleague chase  nordegren he always says that  assumptions are a threat to equity and i  think in this case assumptions could be  a threat to equity because then it can  start to limit the content that our  students are exposed to  and ultimately what we want to do is  make sure that we're exposing our  students to grade level content  irregardless of where their readiness  level might be  and the challenge then for teachers is  how to do that that's the biggest  challenge that was a challenge i faced  throughout my entire career is helping  people understand  how do i keep my students engaged  at levels that honor where they are but  still hold the standard or expectation  for their grade level and i think that  is um  a matter of really getting clear on the  content so  let's break it make it real  yeah if i'm student who  really is still struggling with my  multiplication facts for the life of me  i cannot tell you what seve